# Build output
_build/

# Fetched registry modules and the vendored dependency tree
.repos/
.mooncakes/
.moon/

# Tooling and editor scratch
.arts/
.codeartsdoer/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Python bytecode. The CI scripts under `.github/scripts/` are Python, and
# importing one -- which the README-sync test does -- writes a `__pycache__`
# next to it.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Root-level QA scratch only. The pattern is anchored on purpose: a bare
# `qa/` would also match `scripts/qa/`, and the harness has to be committed.
# The harnesses write their working directories under `_build/qa/`.
/qa/

# The coverage reporter writes its report into the CURRENT directory, so a
# `moon coverage report -f <format>` run leaves these at the repository root.
# They are outputs, not sources, and they are not worth reviewing.
/bisect.coverage
/cobertura.xml
/coveralls.json
/_coverage/

# THE CORPUS STAYS IN THE REPOSITORY; THE PACKAGE DOES NOT CARRY IT.
#
# `moon publish` packs the working tree minus the files the ignore rules
# match, and the registry refuses an archive it cannot read: the 0.1.35
# upload was rejected with "400 Bad Request, detail: Invalid ZIP archive"
# because the package carried 162,703,756 bytes of unpacked content and an
# 80,863,997-byte entry, where the largest archive the registry had
# accepted (0.1.34, read from the registry's own copy) carried 46,274,868
# bytes and a 39,479,846-byte entry.
#
# The RAW TRACES were the reason, and they are the biggest files in the
# tree. The REPORTS, the SIDECARS and the SUMMARIES went with them: the
# corpus is evidence ABOUT this tree, and the repository carries it -- a
# package a reader downloads does not need a copy of the measurements of
# the project that shipped it. Before this rule the package carried nine
# corpus files (the report, the sidecar and the summary of each of the
# three committed corpora); the 0.1.38 package the registry still serves
# has them, and that is its record.
#
# THE RULE IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. An ignore rule matches a file even
# when the file is TRACKED, and the packer leaves every matched file out:
# the nineteen raw traces were tracked AND ignored at the same time, and
# the registry's own copy of 0.1.38 carries none of them. The corpus
# leaves the same way -- `git ls-files --cached --ignored --exclude-standard`
# is what the envelope check measures, and it is git's own answer.
#
# The files stay TRACKED, because an ignore rule does not untrack anything:
# `git ls-files` still lists them, the tree stays clean, and the corpus is
# in the repository's history. A corpus file that is NEW is committed with
# `git add -f` -- a plain `git add` refuses it, because the rule that keeps
# the corpus out of the package is the very rule that makes git ignore it --
# and when one of them is missing from the index, the package envelope check
# says WHICH ignore rule matched it and prints the command that stages it.
/reports/
